I'd have clients send an `Idempotency-Key` header and store it on the order under a unique index scoped to the account. The `INSERT` can then use `ON CONFLICT`, so this needs no separate idempotency table or lock. Scoping the key by account prevents one account from colliding with another account's keys or reading its orders.

```sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN idempotency_key text;
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX orders_account_idempotency_key ON orders (account_id, idempotency_key);
```

```js
router.post("/", async (req, res) => {
  const key = req.get("Idempotency-Key") || null;
  let row = await db.write(
    "INSERT INTO orders (account_id, sku, quantity, idempotency_key) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4) ON CONFLICT (account_id, idempotency_key) DO NOTHING RETURNING *",
    [req.user.accountId, req.body.sku, req.body.quantity, key]
  );
  if (!row) {
    [row] = await db.query(
      "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE account_id = $1 AND idempotency_key = $2",
      [req.user.accountId, key]
    );
    if (row.sku !== req.body.sku || String(row.quantity) !== String(req.body.quantity)) {
      return res.status(422).end();
    }
  }
  res.status(201).json(row);
});
```

A retry with the same key returns the original order with the same 201. A reused key with a different payload gets a 422. This also covers the uncertain `COMMIT` case from earlier: a client that retries with its key gets the existing order instead of a duplicate. Postgres treats NULLs as distinct in a unique index, so requests without the header behave exactly as they do today.

The open decision is whether to require the header. That would break any current client that doesn't send it. Do you control all the callers?

