This access-logger only fires on `res.on("finish")` — i.e., only when a response actually gets sent. From the earlier walkthrough: on Express 4 with no `try/catch` around `await db.write(...)` in either route handler, an unhandled rejection means the response is *never* sent, `finish` never fires, and that request silently vanishes from the logs entirely — the one case you'd most want logged (a failed write) is exactly the case this misses. Closing that requires either wrapping the handlers (or adding `express-async-errors`) so rejections reach a 4-arg error-logging middleware at the bottom of `app.js`, or moving to Express 5. Worth adding regardless:

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(JSON.stringify({ reqId: req.id, err: err.message }));
  res.status(500).end();
});
```

That's the second half — access log for everything that completes, error log for everything that doesn't — and both are single additions in `app.js`, no changes needed in `routes/`.

