  "SELECT u.id, u.email, u.account_id FROM sessions s JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id JOIN accounts a ON a.id = u.account_id WHERE s.token = $1 AND s.expires_at > now() AND a.deleted_at IS NULL",
  [req.cookies && req.cookies.session]
);
```

After the account scoping from before, every route can only reach the caller's own account. So this one check covers them, and the routes don't need their own `deleted_at IS NULL` filters.

The open question is what the soft delete is for. If it is a restore window, you also need a restore path that doesn't go through `sessionUser`, plus a job that hard-deletes rows after the window ends. If nobody will ever restore, a hard delete would be simpler. Which do you want?

I'd add one middleware in `app.js`, registered before `app.use(sessionUser)` on line 11, that writes a log line when the response finishes:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("finish", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
