All three handlers trust `req.params.id` alone and never check it against the caller's account, so any authenticated user can PATCH/DELETE another account or cancel another account's order by guessing an id (IDOR / broken object-level authorization). `req.user.accountId` is already available and trustworthy — it's set server-side in `auth.js` from the session lookup, never from client input.

Fix: add an account-ownership condition to each `WHERE` clause, and treat "no row matched" as a 404 instead of silently returning success.

**`routes/accounts.js`** — `accounts.id` *is* the account id, so the row can only be the caller's own account:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

(Since `:id` can only ever legitimately equal `req.user.accountId` here, you could alternatively drop `:id` from the path entirely and always operate on `req.user.accountId` — simpler and removes the redundant check.)

**`routes/orders.js`** — orders have a separate `account_id` owner column, so scope on that:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

Also check what `db.write` actually returns (looks array-like given `RETURNING *`) — the current code does `res.json(row)` without checking for an empty result, so today a bad id likely returns `200` with an empty/undefined body rather than a clear error; the `if (!row) return res.status(404).end()` addresses that at the same time.

