Both routes have the same bug: they trust `req.params.id` alone in the `WHERE` clause, even though `sessionUser` middleware (auth.js:11) already puts the caller's `accountId` on `req.user` for every request. Any authenticated user can hit `PATCH /accounts/<other-id>` or `PATCH /orders/<other-id>/cancel` and mutate rows they don't own — classic IDOR/broken object-level authorization.

**`routes/accounts.js` (lines 6-18)**

An account's `id` *is* the account, so the fix is to stop trusting the URL param as authoritative — require it to match the caller's own account and add that as a second SQL predicate (defense in depth, not just an `if`):

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

(Since there's a 1:1 between user and account here, you could alternatively drop `:id` from the route entirely and just use `req.user.accountId` — simpler, and removes the param as an attack surface altogether. Worth doing if nothing legitimately needs to address an account by arbitrary id, e.g. no admin-impersonation use case.)

**`routes/orders.js` (lines 14-20)**

Orders have an `account_id` column (set correctly on insert at line 9), but the cancel handler never filters on it:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const [row] = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

**General notes:**
- Both currently do `res.json(row)`/`res.status(204).end()` unconditionally, so a non-owned or nonexistent id currently returns a 200/204 with whatever `db.write` returns (likely empty array) — worth adding the `if (!row) return res.status(404).end()` check regardless, so callers can't distinguish "not yours" from "doesn't exist" (avoids id-enumeration info leak) while still not silently succeeding.
- This is a pattern bug, not a one-off — worth grepping for every other `UPDATE ... WHERE id = $` / `DELETE ... WHERE id = $` in the routes directory to check for the same missing account-scoping.

